# Local secrets and runtime state — never commit
/.env

# Runtime state, at ANY depth — not just the repo root. `dotnet run` resolves the default relative
# TfLensDataRoot against the PROJECT directory, so a dev machine writes src/TfLens/data/, which the
# old root-anchored `/data/` rule did not cover. That directory holds the raw telemetry archive AND
# the ASP.NET Data Protection key ring that encrypts the stored AppManager tokens and the auth
# cookie — none of which may ever be committed.
data/
**/data/
!/database/
/.vs/TfLens.slnx

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# .NET build output — REQ-NFR-016 (owner UAT 2026-08-28).
# These were TRACKED, and that is not a tidiness point. bin/Debug/net10.0/
# TfLens.staticwebassets.runtime.json carries ABSOLUTE static-web-asset content
# roots: a WSL build writes /mnt/c/... + /home/<user>/.nuget/..., a Windows build
# rewrites the same file to C:\1MyCode\... + C:\Users\<user>\.nuget\...
# (both captured on 2026-08-28). Committing that ships one machine's absolute
# paths to another, which is how a static web asset can 404 on one developer's
# box and not another's — the class of failure behind REQ-UI-001. It also made
# every build dirty ~34 tracked files.
#
# ROOT CAUSE, established 2026-08-29. The agent running day-1 wrote this file
# (commit 979265f, 2026-08-26) with sections for the FRAMEWORK's own artifacts
# (.tfcore/, node_modules/, tests/.artifacts/ ...) and NO section for the
# project's own stack — no bin/, no obj/, in a .NET solution it had just
# scaffolded and whose core-config.yaml and four .csproj files it had written
# itself. Commit 80cb71c ("Updated git ignore", 2026-08-27) then swept 1,041
# build-output files into the index; four later commits took it to 1,962.
#
# The responsibility is the agent's, not the template's. The scaffold's template
# should carry stack rules and TF-007 asks upstream for that — but an agent that
# generates a file is responsible for reading it, and "the generator omitted it"
# is not a defence when the generator is the thing you are operating. Whoever
# adds a stack to this repository owns checking that its build output is ignored.
#
# READ THIS BEFORE ASSUMING THE TWO LINES BELOW ARE ENOUGH: a file that is
# already TRACKED is never ignored, whatever this file says. The ~1,962 build
# artefacts already in the index are unaffected by the patterns below — those
# only stop NEW ones being staged. Removing the tracked ones is a one-off the
# owner runs (agents are barred from writing to version control in every mode):
#
#     bash scripts/untrack-build-output.sh          # dry run — shows the command
#     bash scripts/untrack-build-output.sh --run    # removes them from the index
#
# Nothing is deleted from your disk: --cached edits the index only, the files
# stay where they are and the next build reuses them.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
[Bb]in/
[Oo]bj/
/docs/uatissuessc
